-- title:
--   The function field of a Weierstrass curve is K(x,y)
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field and let $W$ be a Weierstrass curve over $K$, i.e. a tuple of coefficients $a_1,a_2,a_3,a_4,a_6$; no nonsingularity hypothesis is imposed. Write $W.\mathrm{toAffine}$ for the associated affine Weierstrass curve, $W.\mathrm{toAffine.CoordinateRing}$ for its coordinate ring, the quotient of the bivariate polynomial ring $K[X][Y]$ by the ideal generated by the Weierstrass polynomial $Y^2+a_1XY+a_3Y-(X^3+a_2X^2+a_4X+a_6)$, with quotient map `WeierstrassCurve.Affine.CoordinateRing.mk`, and $W.\mathrm{toAffine.FunctionField}$ for the fraction field of this coordinate ring. Let $\bar x$ and $\bar y$ be the images in the function field, under the structure map from the coordinate ring, of the classes of `Polynomial.C Polynomial.X` and of `Polynomial.X`, which are the inner and outer variables $X$ and $Y$ of $K[X][Y]$ respectively. The assertion is that the intermediate field of $W.\mathrm{toAffine.FunctionField}/K$ generated by the two-element set $\{\bar x,\bar y\}$, in the sense of `IntermediateField.adjoin`, is the whole function field: $K(\bar x,\bar y)=\top$.
--
--   This is the standard fact $K(W)=K(x,y)$ for a curve given by a Weierstrass equation: the function field is generated over the base field by the two coordinate functions. It is the tool by which $K$-algebra maps out of the function field are pinned down by their values on $\bar x$ and $\bar y$, and it is used in the project in the bound on the degree of the function field over the subfield pulled back along multiplication-type maps, [`WeierstrassCurve.Affine.finrank_fieldRange_mulPull_le`](thm.html#WeierstrassCurve.Affine.finrank_fieldRange_mulPull_le).
